WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.980
ASP.NET Core containers should run with the least privilege needed.

2
00:00:05.080 --> 00:00:11.560
Running as root is often a convenience default, but it grants the process more permission than many applications require.

3
00:00:11.660 --> 00:00:19.900
A non-root runtime reduces the default impact of application compromise or misconfiguration inside the container boundary.

4
00:00:20.180 --> 00:00:25.620
Modern .NET Linux images may include a non-root app user, but Dockerfiles still need review.

5
00:00:25.720 --> 00:00:30.040
The final stage should make it clear which user runs the process.

6
00:00:30.140 --> 00:00:36.540
It is easy to set a user in one stage, change stages, and unintentionally return to a different runtime identity.

7
00:00:36.820 --> 00:00:40.820
Non-root containers may require application and image adjustments.

8
00:00:40.920 --> 00:00:52.040
Ports, file ownership, temporary directories, logs, upload locations, cache paths, certificate locations, and writable directories should all be reviewed.

9
00:00:52.140 --> 00:00:57.500
The application should not assume it can write anywhere in the filesystem or bind privileged ports.

10
00:00:57.780 --> 00:01:01.460
Avoid broad filesystem permissions only to make startup work.

11
00:01:01.560 --> 00:01:07.480
Changing ownership or permissions should be narrow and tied to specific paths the application needs.

12
00:01:07.580 --> 00:01:13.980
If a directory must be writable, the Dockerfile and runtime configuration should make that requirement explicit.

13
00:01:14.260 --> 00:01:22.180
File ownership matters for published output, mounted volumes, generated files, and temporary data.

14
00:01:22.280 --> 00:01:31.240
A container that works in a local development run may fail in production when volumes, orchestrator security context, or read-only filesystem settings differ.

15
00:01:31.340 --> 00:01:34.540
Test the runtime identity under deployment-like conditions.

16
00:01:34.820 --> 00:01:38.100
Least privilege is not a single Dockerfile line.

17
00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:50.280
It is the combined result of runtime user, filesystem permissions, writable paths, exposed ports, container runtime settings, application assumptions, and operational procedures.
